 "chunks": [
  {
   "text": "Over the whole numbers, there's nothing to differentiate.",
   "start": 0.725,
   "end": 4.213
  },
  {
   "text": "Alpöge's note puts it this way: multiplicity is hard to find over the integers,",
   "start": 4.213,
   "end": 9.362
  },
  {
   "text": "corresponding to something like differentiating whole numbers.",
   "start": 9.362,
   "end": 12.925
  },
  {
   "text": "But root counting has an old integer cousin.",
   "start": 13.825,
   "end": 16.712
  },
  {
   "text": "A whole number that isn't zero, but is divisible by many primes,",
   "start": 16.712,
   "end": 20.288
  },
  {
   "text": "must be large.",
   "start": 20.288,
   "end": 21.712
  },
  {
   "text": "Twenty choose ten is divisible by eleven, thirteen,",
   "start": 21.712,
   "end": 25.137
  },
  {
   "text": "seventeen and nineteen, so it's at least their product,",
   "start": 25.137,
   "end": 28.325
  },
  {
   "text": "and it's less than four to the tenth.",
   "start": 28.325,
   "end": 30.512
  },
  {
   "text": "Divisibility plays the role of multiplicity; size plays the role of degree.",
   "start": 30.512,
   "end": 35.537
  },
  {
   "text": "In the 1850s, Chebyshev used this idea, with ratios of factorials,",
   "start": 36.0,
   "end": 41.05
  },
  {
   "text": "to bound how many primes there are, but only roughly:",
   "start": 41.05,
   "end": 44.362
  },
  {
   "text": "those numbers aren't divisible enough.",
   "start": 44.362,
   "end": 46.55
  }
 ],
 "cues": {
